/-! ## (A) THE DRESSED s-TERM IS THE WHOLE-STACK CASIMIR READING (bit-identical, `3/16`). -/

/-- ★★ THE DRESSED s-TERM EQUALS THE WHOLE-STACK CASIMIR READING (identity): dressing the interior
    anti-screening magnitude by `/(1−depthWeight)` gives `projectedS/(1−depthWeight) =
    casimirRatio·(depthWeight/(1−depthWeight))` — the Casimir fraction seen through the WHOLE depth
    stack (weight `w/(1−w)`), not through ONE depth (weight `w`). Pure `Cut` arithmetic from the banked
    `projectedS = casimirRatio·depthWeight` (`projectedS_factorization`) and `depthWeight ≠ 1`. -/
theorem dressedSEff_eq_wholeStack :
    projectedS / (1 - depthWeight)
      = casimirRatio * (depthWeight / (1 - depthWeight)) := by
  rw [projectedS_factorization]
  rw [depthWeight_eq]
  ring

/-- ★★ THE DRESSED s-TERM MAGNITUDE IS `3/16`: `projectedS/(1−depthWeight) = (1/8)/(1−1/3) = 3/16`. -/
theorem dressedSEff_value : projectedS / (1 - depthWeight) = 3 / 16 := by
  rw [projectedS_eq, depthWeight_eq]; norm_num

/-- ★★ THE WHOLE-STACK CASIMIR READING IS `3/16`: `casimirRatio·(depthWeight/(1−depthWeight)) =
    (3/8)·((1/3)/(2/3)) = (3/8)·(1/2) = 3/16`. -/
theorem wholeStack_value :
    casimirRatio * (depthWeight / (1 - depthWeight)) = 3 / 16 := by
  rw [casimirRatio_eq, depthWeight_eq]; norm_num

/-! ## (B) THE DRESSED READING OVERTURNS THE DERIVED `s` — the frame teeth. -/

/-- ★★★ THE DRESSED s-TERM OVERTURNS THE DERIVED `s`: `projectedS/(1−depthWeight) = 3/16 ≠ 1/8 =
    projectedS`. Dressing the anti-screening term re-selects the whole-stack Casimir value `3/16`,
    which is NOT the banked derived one-depth magnitude `s = 1/8` (`projectedS_eq`). So the dressed
    frame contradicts the banked derivation of `s` — it cannot be the consistent composition. -/
theorem dressed_overturns_derivedS : projectedS / (1 - depthWeight) ≠ projectedS := by
  rw [dressedSEff_value, projectedS_eq]; norm_num

/-- ★★ THE WHOLE-STACK WEIGHT IS NOT THE ONE-DEPTH WEIGHT: `depthWeight/(1−depthWeight) = 1/2 ≠ 1/3 =
    depthWeight`. The dressing re-weights the Casimir fraction by the whole-stack weight `w/(1−w) =
